2.47.1

Disable DELAY_DEQUEUE and RUN_TO_PARITY as default.

The Symbiotic Effect of the "Double Negative" - Google/Gemini

To understand why they work so well together, look at how the scheduler
handles a newly awakened interactive task (like a game engine thread,
a mouse click event, or an audio buffer filling) when both features
are disabled:

Scheduler Action

 1. Evaluating the Runqueue

    With Defaut Settings:
      DELAY_DEQUEUE keeps sleeping tasks lingering in the queue to
      recalculate eligibility math. The queue is cluttered.

    NO_DELAY_DEQUEUE + NO_RUN_TO_PARITY
      NO_DELAY_DEQUEUE instantly drops sleeping tasks. The scheduler
      has a perfectly clean, accurate picture of free cores.

 2. Finding a Core

    With Defaut Settings:
      The task is placed on a core via a heavy search path.

    NO_DELAY_DEQUEUE + NO_RUN_TO_PARITY
      Your POC Selector (0290) instantly assigns the task to a
      cache-hot core via your lock-free atomic bitmap.

 3. Claiming the CPU

    With Defaut Settings:
      RUN_TO_PARITY blocks the task from executing if a background
      task is currently running, forcing it to wait until the current
      task hits "parity."

    NO_DELAY_DEQUEUE + NO_RUN_TO_PARITY
      NO_RUN_TO_PARITY violently preempts the running task.
      The interactive thread claims the core and executes immediately.

Why This Combination Specifically Prevents Micro-Stutters

If you disable DELAY_DEQUEUE but leave RUN_TO_PARITY enabled, you
create a new bottleneck:

 1. Your lightning-fast POC Selector will successfully find an
    available execution pipeline instantly.

 2. However, if that pipeline happens to have a running thread on
    it (like a background process), RUN_TO_PARITY steps in as an
    anti-preemption brake. It tells your newly awakened task:
    "I know you found this core quickly, but you must sit and wait
    for this running thread to finish its fair share."

By turning both off, you completely eliminate that handoff latency.
A task is selected instantly via your bitmap (POC patch), wakes up,
and immediately knocks out whatever background noise was occupying
that core.

diff -uarp a/kernel/sched/features.h b/kernel/sched/features.h
--- a/kernel/sched/features.h
+++ b/kernel/sched/features.h
@@ -17,7 +17,7 @@ SCHED_FEAT(PLACE_REL_DEADLINE, true)
  * Inhibit (wakeup) preemption until the current task has either matched the
  * 0-lag point or until is has exhausted it's slice.
  */
-SCHED_FEAT(RUN_TO_PARITY, true)
+SCHED_FEAT(RUN_TO_PARITY, false)
 /*
  * Allow wakeup of tasks with a shorter slice to cancel RUN_TO_PARITY for
  * current.
@@ -55,7 +55,7 @@ SCHED_FEAT(CACHE_HOT_BUDDY, true)
  *
  * DELAY_ZERO clips the lag on dequeue (or wakeup) to 0.
  */
-SCHED_FEAT(DELAY_DEQUEUE, true)
+SCHED_FEAT(DELAY_DEQUEUE, false)
 SCHED_FEAT(DELAY_ZERO, true)
 
 SCHED_FEAT(PARANOID_AVG, false)
